Let $E$ denote the given expression. Apply $\sin x\cos x=\frac{1}{2}\sin(2x)$ at $x=\frac{\pi}{8}$. Dividing by secant multiplies by cosine because $1/\sec x=\cos x$. The resulting pair at $x=\frac{\pi}{4}$ permits a second application.
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{\frac{3}{2}\,\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{4}\right)}{\sec\left(\frac{\pi}{4}\right)}\\&=\frac{3}{2}\,\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{4}\right)\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{4}\right)\\&=\frac{3}{4}\,\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{2}\right)
\end{aligned}
$$
The terminal sine is $\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{2}\right)=1$.
Therefore,
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{3}{4}\,\left(1\right)\\&=\frac{3}{4}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Let $E$ denote the given expression. Apply $\sin x\cos x=\frac{1}{2}\sin(2x)$ at $x=\frac{7 \pi}{16}$. Dividing by secant multiplies by cosine because $1/\sec x=\cos x$. The resulting pair at $x=\frac{7 \pi}{8}$ permits a second application.
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{\frac{3}{2}\,\sin\left(\frac{7 \pi}{8}\right)}{\sec\left(\frac{7 \pi}{8}\right)}\\&=\frac{3}{2}\,\sin\left(\frac{7 \pi}{8}\right)\cos\left(\frac{7 \pi}{8}\right)\\&=\frac{3}{4}\,\sin\left(\frac{7 \pi}{4}\right)
\end{aligned}
$$
The terminal sine is $\sin\left(\frac{7 \pi}{4}\right)=- \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}$.
Therefore,
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{3}{4}\,\left(- \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right)\\&=- \frac{3 \sqrt{2}}{8}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Let $E$ denote the given expression. Apply $\sin x\cos x=\frac{1}{2}\sin(2x)$ at $x=\frac{7 \pi}{24}$ in the denominator. Use $\sec x=1/\cos x$, then apply the double-angle identity again at $x=\frac{7 \pi}{12}$.
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{6\,\sec\left(\frac{7 \pi}{12}\right)}{\sin\left(\frac{7 \pi}{12}\right)}\\&=\frac{6}{\sin\left(\frac{7 \pi}{12}\right)\cos\left(\frac{7 \pi}{12}\right)}\\&=\frac{12}{\sin\left(\frac{7 \pi}{6}\right)}
\end{aligned}
$$
The terminal sine is $\sin\left(\frac{7 \pi}{6}\right)=- \frac{1}{2}$.
Therefore,
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{12}{\left(- \frac{1}{2}\right)}\\&=-24.
\end{aligned}
$$

Let $E$ denote the given expression. Apply $\sin x\cos x=\frac{1}{2}\sin(2x)$ at $x=\frac{5 \pi}{12}$ in the denominator. Use $\sec x=1/\cos x$, then apply the double-angle identity again at $x=\frac{5 \pi}{6}$.
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{2\,\sec\left(\frac{5 \pi}{6}\right)}{\sin\left(\frac{5 \pi}{6}\right)}\\&=\frac{2}{\sin\left(\frac{5 \pi}{6}\right)\cos\left(\frac{5 \pi}{6}\right)}\\&=\frac{4}{\sin\left(\frac{5 \pi}{3}\right)}
\end{aligned}
$$
The terminal sine is $\sin\left(\frac{5 \pi}{3}\right)=- \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}$.
Rationalize the denominator by multiplying numerator and denominator by $\sqrt{3}$:
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{4}{\left(- \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)}\\&=\frac{-8}{\sqrt{3}}\\&=- \frac{8 \sqrt{3}}{3}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Let $E$ denote the given expression. Apply $\sin x\cos x=\frac{1}{2}\sin(2x)$ at $x=\frac{3 \pi}{8}$ in the denominator. Use $\sec x=1/\cos x$, then apply the double-angle identity again at $x=\frac{3 \pi}{4}$.
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{2\,\sec\left(\frac{3 \pi}{4}\right)}{\sin\left(\frac{3 \pi}{4}\right)}\\&=\frac{2}{\sin\left(\frac{3 \pi}{4}\right)\cos\left(\frac{3 \pi}{4}\right)}\\&=\frac{4}{\sin\left(\frac{3 \pi}{2}\right)}
\end{aligned}
$$
The terminal sine is $\sin\left(\frac{3 \pi}{2}\right)=-1$.
Therefore,
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{4}{\left(-1\right)}\\&=-4.
\end{aligned}
$$
